{:edb/db-before-t 2051 :edb/db-after-t 2052 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535364 50 #inst "2026-10-08T22:49:44.891Z" 13194139535364 true] [17592186119661 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345942 13194139535364 true] [17592186119661 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345956 13194139535364 true] [17592186119661 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345963 13194139535364 true] [17592186119663 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345949 13194139535364 true] [17592186119663 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345970 13194139535364 true] [17592186345942 1017 #edb/ref 17592186345943 13194139535364 true] [17592186345942 1089 "q-127219" 13194139535364 true] [17592186345942 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535364 true] [17592186345942 1100 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=1.$" 13194139535364 true] [17592186345942 1113 #uuid "d442f185-bfd4-5e42-90bb-3802ec3f9725" 13194139535364 true] [17592186345942 1145 "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨2,\\,2(1),\\,\\frac{1}{1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+1} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535364 true] [17592186345943 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345944 13194139535364 true] [17592186345943 1000 #edb/ref 1759218