 }{4}$, given that  $p(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=i-\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\sqrt{3}\\,k.$" 13194139535364 true] [17592186345949 1113 #uuid "9a941148-69a9-5b56-a529-c065abef2a52" 13194139535364 true] [17592186345949 1145 "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)\\cdot p(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t\\,p(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})p(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){p}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2(i-\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,k)+(1)(\\sqrt{3}\\,k) \\\\  & =(2\\,i-3\\,j+\\sqrt{3}\\,k)+\\sqrt{3}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i-3\\,j+2\\sqrt{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9+12} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (2\\,i-3\\,j+2\\sqrt{3}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n $T(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{2}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,k.$" 13194139535364 true] [17592186345950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345951 13194139535364 true] [17592186345950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186345952 13194139535364 true] [17